# Visual hierarchy (VH)

This is the short form. Before writing code in this area, read the full rules with reasons, bad/good examples and checks: `.ui-polish/rules/23-visual-hierarchy.md`.

- **VH-01 Each view has one focal point** (warning). Decide, before styling, the single most important thing on each view: the page title and primary action on an app screen, the headline and call to action on a landing page, the key number on a dashboard. Make it the strongest element by at least two of: size, weight, contrast, colour, space around it, position (top left in apps, top centre or left in heroes). Everything else is visibly weaker. Write the focal point of each main view in `design.md` or the task notes so it survives later edits.
- **VH-02 Levels differ enough to be seen** (warning). Adjacent heading levels differ by at least a 1.2× size step (1.25 or more on marketing pages), the page title is at least 1.75× the body size in apps and 2.5× or more in a landing-page hero, and a level that is not bigger is distinguished by weight or colour instead. Do not use two sizes that differ by 1 or 2px: pick from the scale (TY-05) so levels are obvious. Section headings need more space above than below (SP-03) so they attach to their content.
- **VH-03 Three levels of text emphasis** (warning). Text has three emphasis levels, each a token: primary (`--foreground`, for content and headings), secondary (`--muted-foreground`, for supporting text, metadata, descriptions) and disabled or placeholder. Use colour and weight to de-emphasise before reducing size: a date or a helper text gets the secondary colour at body size rather than shrinking to 11px. Every level still meets 4.5:1 contrast (AX-09), so secondary is a mid grey from the tinted neutral scale (CO-02), never an opacity hack on top of an unknown background.
- **VH-04 Values are louder than their labels** (info). In KPI tiles, detail views, summaries and description lists the data is what people came for, so the value is the strong element and the label the quiet one: label in secondary colour at small or body size, value in primary colour, larger or heavier. Do not bold the labels ("**Status:** Paid") or put both at the same weight. When a label is obvious from the value (an email address, a date), drop the label and let format carry it.
- **VH-05 Layout follows how the page is scanned** (warning). Text-heavy and app screens are scanned in an F-pattern: left-aligned headings, labels and the first words of each line carry the meaning, so align content to a left edge (SP-04), front-load headings and put filters and key columns on the left. Sparse marketing sections are scanned in a Z-pattern: logo top left, navigation and the secondary action top right, headline and supporting visual across the middle, primary call to action at the end of the diagonal. Do not centre long text (SL-10), and do not put the key action where the scan pattern never reaches (the far bottom right of a long app page).
- **VH-06 It passes the squint test** (warning). Before calling a screen done, blur the screenshot (squint, or a 4 to 8px blur in an image tool) and check: the focal point is still first; groups are still distinguishable by space and shape; there are at most three or four distinct visual weights; nothing unimportant (a border, a badge, a decorative icon, a coloured background) is among the darkest or most saturated things on the screen. Fix by removing emphasis from the competing elements, not by adding more to the important one.
- **VH-07 Few font weights** (warning). A product uses at most three or four font weights in total, usually regular (400) for text, medium (500) for labels and controls, and semibold (600) or bold (700) for headings, plus at most one display weight on marketing pages. Weight is an emphasis level, not decoration: use the same weight for the same role everywhere (TY-08), and never use light weights (300 or lower) for body text or small sizes.
